{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1653 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7789a222c7b2a0a79e173578865b27d1f95f31f29b1fe05bc3857ffd36fc4d4e" :edb/db-before-t 1652 :edb/db-after-t 1653 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534965 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:51:29.709Z" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075119 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075120 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075121 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075122 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075123 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445938 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445940 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445941 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445946 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445947 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186075118 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445938 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078374 1071 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186078374 1071 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078582 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445956 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078582 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445957 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078582 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445959 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078583 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445958 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078671 1050 660.0 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078707 1071 "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t > 0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is $25^{∘}C$, which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186078707 1071 "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186078707 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186079619 1064 "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, letting [math]t_1[/math] be the time when the thermometer reads [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C},[/math] we have the following:\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n40 &= 20 + 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\n20 &= 80e^{-kt_1} \\\\[5pt]\ne^{-kt_1} &= \\dfrac14 \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac14\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n-kt_1 &= -2\\ln\\left(2\\right) \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{k} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= \\dfrac{2\\ln\\left(2\\right)}{\\dfrac17\\ln\\left(2\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\nt_1 &= 14\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the thermometer will read [math]40^\\circ\\textrm{C}[/math] after [math]14[/math] minutes." 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186079619 1064 "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186079671 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n*The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186079671 1064 "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n> *The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." 13194139534965 17592186045417 false] [17592186445927 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445927 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445927 1083 #uuid "0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445928 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445928 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445928 1083 #uuid "11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445929 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445929 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445929 1083 #uuid "2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445930 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=60{y}^{2}-960" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445930 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445930 1083 #uuid "37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445931 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445931 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445931 1083 #uuid "3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445932 1080 "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+16)" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445932 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445932 1083 #uuid "42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445933 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y-90),\\,y(0)=60" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445933 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445933 1083 #uuid "4815316b-c603-51