592186445956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445956 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445956 1072 #uuid "0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445956 1074 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=5,a=90,$ and ${y}_{0}=130.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=60<a=90.$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445955 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1069 "q-106658" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1072 #uuid "4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445957 1074 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=30,a=200,$ and ${y}_{0}=210.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=100<a=200.$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445952 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1069 "q-106932" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1071 "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1072 #uuid "502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445958 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-16).\n$$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445953 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1069 "q-106652" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1071 "Given that $y>16$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1072 #uuid "daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true] [17592186445959 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-60(y-16).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=60,$ and $a=16.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>16$ for all $t>0.$" 13194139534965 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0f2da53c-c7ca-5c98-8d85-a2aeb6fe3fa6" 17592186445927 "answer-11ee4e76-6dd6-5534-86f3-54f86c2dcfb8" 17592186445928 "answer-2ae1f843-84e5-5dc8-82c0-b0bc4b88a1a2" 17592186445929 "answer-37f8e72f-d006-593d-97e3-d9ede565e54f" 17592186445930 "answer-3e383006-56b2-5f3b-b5e0-6e135886c311" 17592186445931 "answer-42711072-4521-5fe7-9fb9-04bbe0e1aac5" 17592186445932 "answer-4815316b-c603-51a2-a732-458284f7c774" 17592186445933 "answer-5a6c778e-b63c-5aa6-b1ce-5c050c5f7b53" 17592186445934 "answer-5d1b5621-3f57-51c5-806e-e99b647b764b" 17592186445935 "answer-6056756e-9986-54df-b147-e00ce576b435" 17592186445936 "answer-61ae82ec-9879-5f5d-ab92-a1997ae823f6" 17592186445937 "answer-72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" 17592186445938 "answer-7de9a5d8-fca6-54df-be6c-1bc3b035ed8b" 17592186445939 "answer-a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" 17592186445940 "answer-ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" 17592186445941 "answer-b5f10bfc-7725-5838-829c-638f4fde25b9" 17592186445942 "answer-c269d59b-4c24-5a2e-8b68-d56b249ca9ca" 17592186445943 "answer-c77bbc7e-9d55-561a-80a6-ebb8b2046097" 17592186445944 "answer-cddd42ed-edf0-54e7-998a-1f3cc455ccb0" 17592186445945 "answer-e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" 17592186445946 "answer-e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" 17592186445947 "answer-e4e26874-dc7d-53de-91b5-91950a6ae0a7" 17592186445948 "answer-e9fd2ab7-1578-5858-845a-20b1a7944785" 17592186445949 "answer-f46e95ee